===============================
KUBERNETES TROUBLESHOOTING NOTES
===============================

1. POD STUCK IN PENDING (Scheduling Issues)
-------------------------------------------
Reason: Scheduler cannot find a suitable node.

Common Causes:
- Insufficient CPU/Memory resources.
- PVC is not bound or available.
- Node has taints and pod has no tolerations.
- Wrong nodeSelector.
- Affinity/Anti-affinity rules not satisfied.
- Namespace ResourceQuota exceeded.
- Node is NotReady.
- Maximum pod limit reached on node.

Commands:
kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node-name>
kubectl get pvc
kubectl describe quota


2. POD STUCK IN CONTAINERCREATING
---------------------------------
Reason: Pod is scheduled but container cannot start.

Common Causes:
- ErrImagePull.
- ImagePullBackOff.
- Wrong image name or tag.
- Private registry authentication issue.
- Secret not available.
- ConfigMap not found.
- Missing environment variables.
- PVC/Volume mount issues.
- Init container failure.
- Storage attachment failure.

Commands:
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <init-container>
kubectl get secrets
kubectl get configmap
kubectl get pvc


3. POD RUNNING BUT NOT HEALTHY
------------------------------
Reason: Container started but application is failing.

Common Causes:
- CrashLoopBackOff.
- OOMKilled.
- Application crash.
- Wrong startup command.
- Readiness probe failure.
- Liveness probe failure.
- Startup probe failure.
- Resource limits too low.

Commands:
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>


4. APPLICATION ERRORS
---------------------
Reason: Pod is running but application is not working.

Common Causes:
- Database connection issue.
- Service selector mismatch.
- Endpoints not created.
- DNS resolution failure.
- NetworkPolicy blocking traffic.
- Wrong ConfigMap values.
- Wrong Secret values.
- External API unavailable.
- Environment variable issues.

Commands:
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc <service-name>
kubectl get endpoints
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- sh


5. SERVICE ISSUES
-----------------
Reason: Traffic is not reaching the pods.

Common Causes:
- Selector mismatch.
- TargetPort mismatch.
- Service port mismatch.
- No endpoints.
- Backend pods not Ready.

Commands:
kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc <service-name>
kubectl get endpoints


6. INGRESS ISSUES
-----------------
Reason: External traffic cannot reach the application.

Common Causes:
- Ingress controller not running.
- Wrong ingress class.
- Incorrect path rules.
- TLS certificate issue.
- DNS configuration problem.
- LoadBalancer not provisioned.

Commands:
kubectl get ingress
kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name>
kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx


7. NODE ISSUES
--------------
Reason: Worker node problems.

Common Causes:
- Node NotReady.
- Memory pressure.
- Disk pressure.
- PID pressure.
- Network unavailable.
- Kubelet stopped.
- Container runtime failure.

Commands:
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node-name>


8. COMMON POD STATUS ERRORS
---------------------------
Pending:
- Waiting for scheduling.

ContainerCreating:
- Pulling image or mounting volumes.

Running:
- Application is running.

CrashLoopBackOff:
- Application keeps crashing.

ImagePullBackOff:
- Cannot pull container image.

ErrImagePull:
- Image not found or authentication issue.

OOMKilled:
- Container exceeded memory limit.

Completed:
- Job finished successfully.

Terminating:
- Pod is shutting down.


9. COMMON KUBERNETES COMMANDS
-----------------------------
Check pods:
kubectl get pods

Describe pod:
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

View logs:
kubectl logs <pod-name>

View previous logs:
kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous

Execute inside pod:
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- sh

Check services:
kubectl get svc

Check endpoints:
kubectl get endpoints

Check ingress:
kubectl get ingress

Check nodes:
kubectl get nodes

Check PVC:
kubectl get pvc

Check ConfigMaps:
kubectl get configmap

Check Secrets:
kubectl get secrets

Check events:
kubectl get events

Check namespace quotas:
kubectl describe quota


==========================================
PRODUCTION TROUBLESHOOTING FLOW (INTERVIEW)
==========================================

If an application is down:

Step 1:
Check pod status.
kubectl get pods

Step 2:
Describe the pod.
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

Step 3:
Check logs.
kubectl logs <pod-name>

Step 4:
Check cluster events.
kubectl get events

Step 5:
Verify PVCs.
kubectl get pvc

Step 6:
Verify Service and Endpoints.
kubectl get svc
kubectl get endpoints

Step 7:
Verify Ingress or LoadBalancer.
kubectl get ingress

Step 8:
Check node health.
kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node-name>

Step 9:
Verify ConfigMaps and Secrets.
kubectl get configmap
kubectl get secrets

Step 10:
Verify application connectivity and database connections.


